<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope John XII</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/955">955</a>–964 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope John XII was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the mid-tenth century. In 962 CE he crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I of Saxony</a> as emperor. Historians generally treat this coronation as the functional founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-john-xii.jpg" alt="Pope John XII" width="147" height="261" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Otto_I_begegnet_Papst_Johannes_XII_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Octavian was the son of Alberic II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a>, patrician and self-styled prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. His mother is believed to have been Alda of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a>, daughter of King Hugh of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, though there is some doubt, and Benedict of Soracte recorded him as the son of a concubine. Before Alberic's death in 954 he made the Roman nobles swear that the next papal vacancy would go to his son. Octavian succeeded his father as prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and, after the death of Agapetus II, was elected pope on 16 December 955, adopting the name John XII. He issued secular directives as Octavian and church documents as John.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Around 960 John led an attack on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> duchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Beneventum</a> and Capua, but retreated and made a treaty with Gisulf I of Salerno at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terracina">Terracina</a>. Attacked by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berengar-ii-of-italy">Berengar II of Italy</a> and unable to control the Roman nobility, he appealed to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Otto entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 31 January 962, and John crowned him emperor, the first in the West since the death of Berengar I almost 40 years before. Eleven days later they ratified the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diploma-ottonianum">Diploma Ottonianum</a>, which made the emperor guarantor of the independence of the Papal States and confirmed the freedom of papal elections, while the emperor kept the right to agree to an election before consecration.</p><p>In 960 he confirmed Saint Dunstan as archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>, and at a synod on 12 February 962 he agreed to found the Archbishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>. After Otto left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, John sent envoys to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> to form a league against him and negotiated with Adalbert, the son of Berengar. Otto besieged Rome in the summer of 963. John fled to Tibur with the papal treasury, and a council summoned by Otto deposed him and elected Leo VIII.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When Otto left, John returned in February 964, and a synod he summoned declared his deposition uncanonical. He died on 14 May 964. Liudprand of Cremona, a partisan of Otto, recorded the charges brought at the Synod of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 963, among them simony, ordaining a ten-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, and adultery, and much of the later condemnation of John rests on them. Ratherius of Verona also wrote of immoral conduct. The historian Ferdinand Gregorovius judged him leniently, pointing to his youth and the anomalous position of prince and pope at once, and his enemies used the accusations to obscure the political side of his deposition.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pope John XII” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…fragmentation of the Carolingian realm the imperial succession lapsed and was contested until Otto I of Saxony, having defeated the Magyars at the Battle of Lechfeld in 955 CE, was crowned emperor by <b>Pope John XII</b> in 962 CE, the date most historians treat as the functional founding.</blockquote><a href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The newly crowned emperor Otto I issued a charter to <b>Pope John XII</b> confirming the papacy's possession of the lands granted by the Carolingians.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-13">13 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In Rome, <b>Pope John XII</b> solemnly crowned Otto I emperor.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-2">2 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the authority of Emperor Otto I, a possibly uncanonical synod met in Saint Peter's Basilica to depose <b>Pope John XII</b>, who had crowned Otto emperor in 962, for armed rebellion against him.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-6">6 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=959" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Pope John XII"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5788 3947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5788 3947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=959">What was here in 959 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
